  "summary": "Refined the service-level restart approach: wire `on_restart` in tui.py's ServerClient instantiation to an async `_handle_remote_restart` method that (1) checks if the systemd service unit exists at `~/.config/systemd/user/<service_name>.service` using `service_name_for_dir` from service.py — if not installed, send an error back via WebSocket so the webui can display 'Service not installed, cannot restart' rather than failing silently; (2) if installed, performs a minimal graceful shutdown (release `.pipeline-lock.tui`, set `kill_requested` on any active agent statuses to abort in-progress work) before calling `subprocess.Popen(['systemctl', '--user', 'restart', service_name], start_new_session=True)` — the `start_new_session=True` is critical so the systemctl process survives the parent TUI being killed by the restart. The previous proposals correctly identified that the Go server doing the restart would be cleaner (it's a separate process), but the server's Config struct has no project directory field and clients don't send their project path — adding that plumbing is more invasive than the TUI self-restart approach and not worth it for this fix. For the webui: the fetch response will likely never arrive since the TUI (and thus WebSocket) dies during restart, so the JS should show 'Restart initiated...' immediately on fetch success (HTTP 200 from the Go server confirming the signal was sent), then detect the WebSocket disconnect and show a reconnecting indicator rather than relying on the .then() chain. Convert both client.html and index.html from native confirm()/alert() to the existing `.modal-overlay` pattern for the confirmation dialog, and use showToast() for feedback (adding showToast to client.html which currently lacks it). The error-path alert() calls at index.html line 1097 and client.html line 643/648 must also be converted."
}
